WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.894 --> 00:00:14.552
Now I'd like to introduce Jan Pietzsch,
who is today's featured speaker.

00:00:14.552 --> 00:00:16.257
Dr. Pietzsch's professional and

00:00:16.257 --> 00:00:19.710
academic work focuses on early
stage technology assessment.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.166
And strategic decision support for
investors, manufacturers and

00:00:23.166 --> 00:00:24.503
research institutions.

00:00:24.503 --> 00:00:28.561
A primary emphasis is on evaluating the
value proposition of new technologies, and

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:32.680
providing quantitative inputs into the
selection of promising technology concepts

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.995
and their targeted development.

00:00:34.995 --> 00:00:38.251
These includes model based
assessment of the clinical and

00:00:38.251 --> 00:00:41.783
health economic benefit from
new diagnostics and therapies.

00:00:41.783 --> 00:00:45.420
He received his academic training at
the University of Karlsruhe in Germany.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.184
And also here at Stanford University
where he completed his PhD.

00:00:49.184 --> 00:00:53.649
Dr. Pietzsch is co-founder,
president, and CEO of WingTech Inc.

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:56.715
That's the technology consulting
firm focused on health economic and

00:00:56.715 --> 00:00:58.360
value assessment.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.323
Previously, he'd been a research
fellow with the US FDA's Office of

00:01:02.323 --> 00:01:03.539
Device Evaluation.

00:01:03.539 --> 00:01:06.450
Currently, he holds an appointment
as Adjunct Professor of

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.905
Management Science and
Engineering at Stanford,

00:01:08.905 --> 00:01:13.330
where he also serves as a director at
the Stanford Bio Center for Biodesign.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:16.880
The center is located here on campus
next to the medical school and

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.500
in the center of Silicon Valley,
which seems fitting.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.940
And it's a leading program in
medical technology innovation.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:23.873
It's vision is for

00:01:23.873 --> 00:01:27.957
a world where health technology innovators
from around the globe have immersive,

00:01:27.957 --> 00:01:31.413
collaborative, cutting edge training
that they require to thrive.

00:01:31.413 --> 00:01:35.259
And on that note,
I'm going to hand over to Jan.

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:36.490
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much Marsha.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:41.780
Good morning everyone, it's a pleasure
to see many of you registered here.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:46.760
And it's also nice to see the interest in
medical technology because it's really,

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.040
I think, one of the fields where you
can combine different interest and

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.870
area competencies and expertise and
really do something very meaningful

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:58.210
by bringing technologies to patients
that really improve outcomes and

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:04.660
that also help to manage care, and
provide services in an appropriate manner.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:05.974
Which is increasingly,

00:02:05.974 --> 00:02:09.294
as we see, important in healthcare
systems around the globe.

00:02:09.294 --> 00:02:12.250
We have two topics today that
I'd like to discuss with you.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:18.178
One is regulation of medical devices and
the second is economic value assessment.

00:02:18.178 --> 00:02:22.127
They are really critical for
the successful bench to bedside innovation

00:02:22.127 --> 00:02:26.130
process of medical technology and
so we'd like to highlight those.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.545
In this webinar there is also a class we
will mention later that I'm teaching in

00:02:29.545 --> 00:02:32.716
the spring time for those of you
interested in taking the course online.

00:02:36.192 --> 00:02:40.280
To set the stage let's look at
a couple of medical technologies.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:46.310
Starting from top left here, you see
a trans-catheter aortic heat valve,

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:51.280
or which is a new way to place and
artificial heart valve.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.078
It replaces the surgical
open chest approach and

00:02:54.078 --> 00:02:58.319
is one of the key cardiovascular
innovations of the last couple of years.

00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:02.165
It really facilitated treatment of
patients that were previously not

00:03:02.165 --> 00:03:05.760
candidates for surgery and
needed to be medically managed only.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:11.060
And also it's a candidate who
are a technology that replaces over

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:11.910
time surgeries.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:17.920
So very high patient value device and
we get back to that later in a case study.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.380
You see on the right, next to it,
deep brain simulation.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.630
Which is also an exciting neural
simulation technology that is used for

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.640
treatment of epilepsy, for example.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.715
For treatment of depression.

00:03:31.715 --> 00:03:34.874
But also for
treatment of Parkinson's Disease.

00:03:34.874 --> 00:03:38.710
So a big patient impact and also,
one of the medical devices.

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:43.260
That they contribute to reduction and
the need for medication usage.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.768
So you see a direct link actually for

00:03:45.768 --> 00:03:51.129
medical technology also can replace
pharmaceutical treatment regimens.

00:03:51.129 --> 00:03:56.117
Next to it you see an advanced stem
therapy for cardiovascular applications.

00:03:56.117 --> 00:03:59.413
You see an imaging device for
mammography or

00:03:59.413 --> 00:04:04.410
breast cancer screening,
which also has a very important field.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.900
I mean, all of imaging and diagnostics.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:10.663
You see just next to it,
further below, a treatment for

00:04:10.663 --> 00:04:14.600
obstructive sleep apnea,
which is a big health concern.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.040
I mean,
obstructive sleep apnea is a condition,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.590
because it really elevates long-term
cardiovascular and stroke risk.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:23.870
And in patients who don't
tolerate CPAP treatment

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:27.309
which is a mask based treatment
to treat sleep apnea.

00:04:27.309 --> 00:04:31.849
A new device has been developed that
again uses neurostimulation technology to

00:04:31.849 --> 00:04:35.790
stimulate the tongue base at night and
thereby keep the airway open.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:42.741
Then you see a diagnostic device for
glucose monitoring which you can

00:04:42.741 --> 00:04:48.213
see over the counter or can purchase
in any pharmacy in the United States,

00:04:48.213 --> 00:04:52.372
certainly at the large need,
or large number of users.

00:04:52.372 --> 00:04:57.520
But their continued advancements,
also with the connection to mobile health

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:03.296
technologies that really facilitate better
management of the condition of diabetes.

00:05:03.296 --> 00:05:07.631
Then next it, to the left,
you see a new approach to actually

00:05:07.631 --> 00:05:12.170
diagnosis lung cancer and
to avoid invasive procedures.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:13.615
It's a genomic marker test.

00:05:13.615 --> 00:05:17.127
You see here a bronchoscopy
being performed and

00:05:17.127 --> 00:05:22.920
a brush that is used to take a sample
which is then analyzed in a lab.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.740
To determine the risk or
the likelihood that

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.960
the patient actually does have
a cancerous condition or a lesion.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:33.110
And by using such new technology there

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:38.100
is the opportunity to reduce
invasive surgical biopsy.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.690
And you can imagine that that's really
a risk reduction for the patent and

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:42.630
it's a benefit also for

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:46.680
the healthcare system in terms of
avoiding costs of complex procedures.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:51.768
Then a device that is a family of devices,
artificial joints,

00:05:51.768 --> 00:05:56.632
you see the hip joint here excuse
me the knee joint, roughly 700,000

00:05:56.632 --> 00:06:01.506
total knee replacements are performed
per year in the United States alone.

00:06:01.506 --> 00:06:08.200
And again, big impact on patient quality
of life in patients with severe arthritis.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:12.788
But also a big market for medical
technology or orthopedic technologies.

00:06:12.788 --> 00:06:18.361
And then finally, technology that's
a high flow therapy respiratory support

00:06:18.361 --> 00:06:23.686
systems that are gradually replacing
more invasive respiratory support,

00:06:23.686 --> 00:06:29.340
mordalities, such as antiobasin
other wise mask based treatments.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.660
Again, I think all of these
technologies and there are many,

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:34.910
many more show you the wide
variety of technologies and

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:39.490
the wide variety of opportunities
to really improve patient care and

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:43.360
patient outcomes.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:48.300
Now for all of these technologies,
if you're thinking of innovations in those

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.980
spaces, right,
we need to think about demonstrating

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:55.180
the value of these technologies to
the patients or at least developing and

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:59.810
understanding, right, how meaningful
a technology is to patients.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:02.760
We also need to think about
the questions of safety and

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:04.100
effectiveness of these technologies.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:10.400
And the leading question, our lead
question really is what constitutes safety

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:11.890
of a medical technology?

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:14.530
What does it take to demonstrate
safety and effectiveness?

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:20.600
Next do we need to worry or
think about the target population.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:24.468
If there's a very narrow defined
population that could benefit

00:07:24.468 --> 00:07:28.921
from the therapy or the technology,
or is it a larger target population.

00:07:28.921 --> 00:07:33.860
Thinking of the knee replacement,
$700,000 per year number of devices.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.370
That's a very different to
some other technology areas.

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:39.929
So we need to appreciate and
define the target population,

00:07:39.929 --> 00:07:43.131
because it also has an affect
on the safety profile and

00:07:43.131 --> 00:07:47.138
on the effectiveness profile and
we'll discuss that in a minute.

00:07:47.138 --> 00:07:51.043
And then finally,
is this technology of value, right?

00:07:51.043 --> 00:07:55.355
Should it be paid for, and if so,
What amount would be appropriate for

00:07:55.355 --> 00:07:56.756
healthcare systems.

00:07:56.756 --> 00:08:00.526
And we also need to keep in mind that
this value question really is stakeholder

00:08:00.526 --> 00:08:02.698
specific, and it's country specific.

00:08:02.698 --> 00:08:05.307
So a technology that might be
of value in the United States.

00:08:05.307 --> 00:08:10.883
Healthcare system might not be of value
in other healthcare systems like the UK,

00:08:10.883 --> 00:08:13.780
France, Spain, or Japan.

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:17.460
And the reason is that we not only
might have some slight variations

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.490
in the underlying clinical profile.

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:23.007
But we have very different
implementations of healthcare systems and

00:08:23.007 --> 00:08:25.638
also different cost structures and
cost profiles.

00:08:25.638 --> 00:08:29.792
And so you see that all of these questions
really directly relate to the fields of

00:08:29.792 --> 00:08:32.900
regulation and
health economics that we discussed today.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:39.412
And a point here that we should
mention specifically here is that very

00:08:39.412 --> 00:08:43.997
different from other industries
like the automotive industry or

00:08:43.997 --> 00:08:46.603
many consumer electronics fields.

00:08:46.603 --> 00:08:51.220
Commercial success and bringing
a therapy really to the patient for

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:55.756
commercial use means that we need
to rely on approval by regulatory

00:08:55.756 --> 00:09:00.454
authorities in the United States
the Food and Drug Administration.

00:09:00.454 --> 00:09:05.332
And we also need to obtain reasonable
positive reimbursement that

00:09:05.332 --> 00:09:09.176
actually allows us to
a business in the end.

00:09:09.176 --> 00:09:14.433
So without those third party
considerations or approval by third

00:09:14.433 --> 00:09:20.834
parties, there's no way to have a
successful translation from development or

00:09:20.834 --> 00:09:24.860
R&amp;D to the clinical use and
the patient benefit.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:29.804
So that means we need to understand very
thoroughly the requirements of regulatory

00:09:29.804 --> 00:09:34.710
approval, and of reimbursement
approval and value assessment.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:39.762
And there's a direct link as you probably
appreciate between the requirements and

00:09:39.762 --> 00:09:44.379
the commercialization risks and
rewards of the time to market, imagine for

00:09:44.379 --> 00:09:49.239
example, a required two year followup
study as opposed to six month followup.

00:09:49.239 --> 00:09:51.899
That very much affects
your time to market and

00:09:51.899 --> 00:09:56.270
it also affects the costs of
development and clinical testing.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:59.900
And that leads us directly also
to the funding requirement,

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:03.968
when you think of raising funds for
a new concept or new technology.

00:10:03.968 --> 00:10:06.891
The questions of what
aspects of regulation and

00:10:06.891 --> 00:10:11.870
reimbursement really drive heavily
the funding requirement question as well.

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:17.869
And there's an increasing importance
globally to demonstrate value in

00:10:17.869 --> 00:10:20.130
a changing healthcare environment.

00:10:22.437 --> 00:10:24.190
So let's talk about regulation.

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:30.407
Couple of years ago, we did a study at
Stanford Biodesign where we outlined

00:10:30.407 --> 00:10:35.635
the medical development process from the
very early stage of need identification,

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:40.220
all the way through post market and
you see, key activities listed here.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.510
You see also decision gates along the way.

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:48.160
And what was really striking, is that
more than half of the activities and

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:51.850
the decisions are governed by regulatory
and reimbursement requirements.

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:54.706
Most of them actually
regulatory requirements.

00:10:54.706 --> 00:10:59.069
Second point I'd like to make here is
that regulation not only relates to

00:10:59.069 --> 00:11:04.020
the approval of medical technologies or
the pre market review, as it's called.

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:08.207
But also to the full life
cycle of a medical device.

00:11:08.207 --> 00:11:12.701
So all aspects of surveillance in
the field managing feedback that

00:11:12.701 --> 00:11:18.520
you received from patients and
providers about issues with the device.

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:22.019
Manufacturing, productions,
all of those aspects are regulated and

00:11:22.019 --> 00:11:23.369
governed by regulations.

00:11:23.369 --> 00:11:28.419
And so In order to successfully manage
a business, you need to be aware

00:11:28.419 --> 00:11:33.230
of those requirements, and
implement procedures accordingly.

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:39.278
Two important definitions that govern
FDA's work when evaluating technology,

00:11:39.278 --> 00:11:44.450
you see the legal definitions from
the Code of Federal Regulations for

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:47.620
Safety and for Effectiveness.

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:49.000
And let me just read them to you.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:53.333
There's reasonable assurance that a device
is safe when it can be determined based on

00:11:53.333 --> 00:11:56.223
valid scientific evidence
that the probable benefits to

00:11:56.223 --> 00:11:58.885
health from use of the device for
its intended uses.

00:11:58.885 --> 00:12:02.624
And conditions of use when accompanied
by adequate directions and

00:12:02.624 --> 00:12:06.140
warnings against unsafe use,
outweigh the probable risks.

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:10.980
Again, in the definition of safety
you see this balance of benefits and

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:13.610
risks, or that the benefits
need to outweigh the risks.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.530
You also see a couple of
important definitions.

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:22.430
One is the question of what
constitutes valid scientific evidence.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:25.050
Is it a randomized control?

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:28.700
Try it, for example,
with the highest level of evidence.

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:30.530
Or it is sufficient,
for some technologies,

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:34.990
to use less comprehensive data to use,
for example a single arm study,

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:39.440
and compare the results to previously
published results from another technology.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:43.710
So this is a very relevant and
key question.

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:46.620
And also is actually one of the drivers

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.770
in the implementation of
the regulatory process.

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.816
You may have heard about
the regulatory pendulum.

00:12:52.816 --> 00:12:56.852
That there were differences between how
to administrations actually enforced

00:12:56.852 --> 00:12:57.579
regulation.

00:12:57.579 --> 00:13:01.881
And implemented regulation as one of
the key drivers here is the question,

00:13:01.881 --> 00:13:06.400
how much evidence is needed and what is
considered to be valid or valid enough?

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:11.140
Another definition is the intended use.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:13.930
You cannot get clearance for a technology.

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:18.270
For use in any patient,
it's always for one specific or

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:21.110
several specific indications per
user intended to do with this.

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:24.580
And you also need to be
careful in describing and

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:26.717
specifying what is the condition of use.

00:13:26.717 --> 00:13:30.924
Is it in an inpatient setting and an
outpatient setting, who is performing or

00:13:30.924 --> 00:13:34.097
using the device,
all of those are relevant questions for

00:13:34.097 --> 00:13:36.490
the regulatory review process.

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:40.520
Labeling is part of that,
as well as the instructions for use.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.890
Effectiveness, looking further below,

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:47.771
there is reasonable assurance that
a device is defective when it can be

00:13:47.771 --> 00:13:51.023
determined based upon again
valid scientific evidence.

00:13:51.023 --> 00:13:55.159
At any significant portion of the target
population the use of the device for

00:13:55.159 --> 00:14:00.004
it's intended use and conditions of use
when adequately to the warnings against

00:14:00.004 --> 00:14:03.250
unsafe use they provide
clinically significant results.

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:09.566
Again definitions of valid
scientific evidence.

00:14:09.566 --> 00:14:14.933
Also definitions for
intended use in a given population and

00:14:14.933 --> 00:14:21.810
also the definition of clinically
significant results that means the FDA.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:25.860
For example, the European regulatory
requirement FDA really evaluates

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:30.530
the benefit to the patient in terms of
outcome, in terms of effectiveness.

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:35.795
It's not sufficient to agree that
a technology's safe enough, but

00:14:35.795 --> 00:14:41.348
it really needs to demonstrate
a clinically significant improvement.

00:14:41.348 --> 00:14:46.206
In Europe, the focus with the labeling,
for those interested and

00:14:46.206 --> 00:14:51.235
familiar with the European system Is
to evaluate safety, and performance.

00:14:51.235 --> 00:14:55.041
And performance means,
not the clinical performance, but

00:14:55.041 --> 00:14:59.366
that revise does what it's intended to do,
but there's no long term

00:14:59.366 --> 00:15:03.110
evaluation of outcomes as part
of the regulatory process.

00:15:05.710 --> 00:15:09.260
How is regulation implemented
in the United States?

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:12.770
When Congress enacted medical
device regulation in 1976,

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:16.510
following pharmaceutical regulation,
which was in place since the 1930s,

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:19.050
with the Food, Drug, and Cosmetics Act.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:23.500
Congress determined that for medical
devices and, again, differential pharma,

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:26.410
it does make sense to consider
the risk level of a device.

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:29.456
Because, you can imagine,
medical devices like syringes.

00:15:29.456 --> 00:15:34.521
And you see the photo here on the right,
have a much different

00:15:34.521 --> 00:15:39.006
risk profile than a intracardiac stint or
catheter.

00:15:39.006 --> 00:15:45.020
And so the three risks, one, two,
three, increasing in risk level.

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:48.277
And the review requirement are different.

00:15:48.277 --> 00:15:53.818
There are exempt devices that really
require only the registration

00:15:53.818 --> 00:16:00.740
of the manufacturer but no submission
of clinical or other Test data.

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:03.520
Then there's the so-called 510(k) process.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:07.500
The definition actually goes back to
the Court of Federal Regulation section.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:11.490
It's the typical process for
Class Two devices, and it requires

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:15.670
demonstration of substantial equivalent
to one or several predicate devices.

00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:20.007
So as long as you can show that your
technology performs similarly to

00:16:20.007 --> 00:16:23.966
an existing technology, or
uses technology components that

00:16:23.966 --> 00:16:27.849
were previously cleared
through the 510(k) process,

00:16:27.849 --> 00:16:32.492
you can demonstrate would be upper
review by FDA, you can demonstrate if

00:16:32.492 --> 00:16:37.247
you're successful that the technology's
substantially equivalent.

00:16:37.247 --> 00:16:41.299
And therefore should be cleared
to market via the 510(k) process.

00:16:41.299 --> 00:16:46.672
Then there's pre-market approval or PMA,
which is the highest regulatory process.

00:16:46.672 --> 00:16:52.175
It's very much comparable to
the new drug application process.

00:16:52.175 --> 00:16:55.480
And medical devices always
require a trial-based evaluation,

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:58.130
typically with several
phases of clinical trials.

00:17:00.404 --> 00:17:04.550
In addition to pre-market evaluation and
clearance as I mentioned earlier,

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:08.195
there are regulations governing
manufacturing and other aspects,

00:17:08.195 --> 00:17:13.161
quality system regulations, R&amp;D processes
manufacturing, procurement distribution,

00:17:13.161 --> 00:17:15.701
product surveillance and
many other aspects.

00:17:19.132 --> 00:17:22.732
FDA review was actually
a paper-based review,

00:17:22.732 --> 00:17:27.100
I mean even though lot of these
papers are now electronic.

00:17:27.100 --> 00:17:31.690
But it's not that FDA evaluates a device,
takes it apart and

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:33.640
studies its risk profile.

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:37.899
Companies submit documentation of
tests that were performed, and

00:17:37.899 --> 00:17:43.680
you see how comprehensive these dockets
are, this is for a PMA-type device.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:48.740
And what FDA receives and then needs
to review within 180 days typically.

00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:50.960
And you can imagine that
that's a tall order, and

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:56.400
a very comprehensive task involving
biostatistics, engineering,

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:00.339
medical disciplines, and so on, and
we expert knowledge within the agency.

00:18:03.103 --> 00:18:04.650
As mentioned earlier,

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:09.687
the implementation of the regulatory
review process has changed over time.

00:18:09.687 --> 00:18:14.147
This show is a shot comes from
the latest MDUFA report that FDA

00:18:14.147 --> 00:18:17.390
published just early this year.

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:20.960
And it shows the pre-market approval,
again the highest risk

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:25.490
does divides approvals, and
you'll see in green the successful or

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:30.120
approved devices and
how that's changed over the years.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:35.170
And you can see, for example, with
the beginning of the Obama administration

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:41.430
in 2008 and 2009, the approval rates for
PMAs really went down significantly.

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:45.760
And that was the results of stricter
requirements that the new administration

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:49.390
or the new leadership at FDA
implemented at that time.

00:18:49.390 --> 00:18:52.760
Since then it has dramatically
increased again, and

00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:57.100
FDA has been very successful actually
in further improving its operations and

00:18:57.100 --> 00:19:01.240
procedures, which is good news to
everyone involved in the industry.

00:19:04.296 --> 00:19:10.284
Now let's switch over to our second topic,
health-economic value assessment,

00:19:10.284 --> 00:19:14.816
to give you some background about
the environment that we all or

00:19:14.816 --> 00:19:18.510
anyone operating in the field
of medical devices.

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:22.585
Now, in these two phase, you see for
the United States, again,

00:19:22.585 --> 00:19:28.820
the total federal spending is percent of
the Gross Domestic Product over the years.

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:34.450
And you can appreciate or see here in red,
the major head programs, that's Medicare

00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:41.240
and Medicaid, primarily being the highest
spending category of government spending.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:43.030
Roughly 5% of the GDP and

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:47.090
consider that this is only
the government part of health care.

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:50.220
When you add all of the private aspects,
and

00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:55.320
commercial insurance aspects of health
care, you're currently looking at 18 or

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:58.030
19% of the the GDP being spent on
health care in the United States,

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:02.430
which is roughly double what
other countries in Europe spend.

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:07.400
And it's not only in Europe, but
especially in the US it's a concern,

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:10.600
especially when you look at
the projected future expenses,

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:15.160
which are driven among
other factors by changes in

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:19.750
the age groups, for example,
of patients, demographic changes.

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:24.450
And you can also appreciate that's
just occurred or what's proposed.

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:29.610
If defense spending, which you see
further down, is increased by $50 billion

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:34.670
from one year to the other at the expense
of other government spending categories,

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:40.585
that there is even more pressure on
spending on healthcare for example.

00:20:40.585 --> 00:20:44.720
Right, and that means also,
there needs to be significant scrutiny,

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:48.720
and funds should be spent
wisely on technologies or

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:52.540
aspects of healthcare that really
are meaningful and contribute value.

00:20:52.540 --> 00:20:54.340
And that's the environment we live in and

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:56.390
that we are increasingly
facing going forward.

00:21:00.966 --> 00:21:04.551
Therefore, not only in
the United States but globally,

00:21:04.551 --> 00:21:08.810
any healthcare system,
regardless of their ability to spend, and

00:21:08.810 --> 00:21:11.900
the absolute amount of
spending on health care.

00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:15.630
All health care systems face
the issue of improving or

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:18.110
the desire to further
improve patient outcomes,

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:22.910
while also managing costs, controlling
costs, in some cases, reducing costs.

00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:27.160
And so technology really can
contribute significantly to helping

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:28.430
resolve these issues, right?

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:33.150
We just need to make sure that
the technology we're bringing forward

00:21:33.150 --> 00:21:36.849
really address one or
two of these concerns.

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:39.100
Ideally both of them, right,

00:21:39.100 --> 00:21:42.830
in outcome improvement and
overall same or reduced cost.

00:21:45.545 --> 00:21:47.450
Now when we look further at value,

00:21:47.450 --> 00:21:52.010
we need to appreciate that there
are different stakeholders involved.

00:21:52.010 --> 00:21:55.093
It's not sufficient to only look
at Medicare, for example, and

00:21:55.093 --> 00:21:59.365
show that Medicare expenses, even though
that's very important and relevant, right?

00:21:59.365 --> 00:22:04.476
If Medicare expenses are reduced with
the introduction of a new technology,

00:22:04.476 --> 00:22:09.271
it might be that it comes at increased
cost, or lower revenue margins for

00:22:09.271 --> 00:22:10.950
the hospitals, right?

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:14.700
And so, from a hospital perspective,
from a pure financial perspective,

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:18.730
that would mean that it's not of
value to adopt a new technology.

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:22.610
And so keeping in mind that
the argument driver should always be

00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:25.410
an improvement in patient outcomes,
but on the financial side, we need

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:29.710
to be aware of those ramifications, and
be aware of different stakeholders.

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:34.013
We need to think about demonstrating
incremental improvement,

00:22:34.013 --> 00:22:35.663
compare the status quo.

00:22:35.663 --> 00:22:38.243
Which also means that
we need to identify and

00:22:38.243 --> 00:22:42.010
very carefully appreciate how
technologies are currently, or

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:45.580
how patients treated with
existing technologies, right?

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:48.450
And we need to identify
the incremental changes.

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:51.067
We need to have a focus on cost and
outcome,

00:22:51.067 --> 00:22:53.627
demonstrate the long term perspective.

00:22:53.627 --> 00:22:56.519
And consider not only the episode of care,
but

00:22:56.519 --> 00:23:00.537
also the longer term implications
in terms of costs and outcomes

00:23:05.602 --> 00:23:11.404
So from a patient perspective, again,
going back to the clinical objectives of

00:23:11.404 --> 00:23:16.608
any new technology, we need to be able
to demonstrate either a reduction

00:23:16.608 --> 00:23:21.901
mortality survival improvement, or
a reduction in patient morbidity,

00:23:21.901 --> 00:23:25.820
or a improvement in patient
related quality of life.

00:23:25.820 --> 00:23:26.950
In some technologies, actually,

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:30.230
we're able to address all of them,
but keep in mind that

00:23:30.230 --> 00:23:34.240
these are the three drivers really that
drive the value proposition to a patient.

00:23:35.670 --> 00:23:39.620
And we need to be able to demonstrate
a viable economic profile,

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:44.420
a therapy needs to be either cost-saving
or cost-effective to be of value.

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:47.290
And that is measured through
the cost-effectiveness ratio,

00:23:47.290 --> 00:23:51.410
on the right-hand side you see a graph
where you see at the zero point or

00:23:51.410 --> 00:23:54.190
the cross of the axis,
the existing approach and

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:58.280
technology, how a patient might be
treated and given an indication, and

00:23:58.280 --> 00:24:00.260
we are looking at The difference in costs.

00:24:00.260 --> 00:24:02.630
There's our costs on the y axis.

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:04.520
And the difference in outcomes or

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:08.470
effectiveness of measured in quality
adjusted life here is to the right.

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:11.170
And you can appreciate that
there might be technologies

00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:15.210
that lead to better patient
outcomes at higher overall costs.

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:19.420
And up to a certain willingness to pay
threshold that is defined by individual

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:23.650
healthcare systems, the technology might
be considered to be of good value,

00:24:23.650 --> 00:24:25.450
to be cost effective.

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:27.700
And that means it should be adopted.

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:31.360
There are also technologies
that hopefully in the future,

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:34.378
more technologies that
improve patient outcomes.

00:24:34.378 --> 00:24:37.240
But also reducing overall cost
to the healthcare system, and

00:24:37.240 --> 00:24:41.500
it would be in the quadrant
on the lower right.

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:45.373
When we think of developing technology
that leads to higher costs, and

00:24:45.373 --> 00:24:50.149
many, many, increased effectiveness that
lie above the willingness to pay threshold

00:24:50.149 --> 00:24:54.730
here, then you can appreciate that they're
not considered to be of good value, and

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:58.991
would be very hard to actually get a
successful adoption of those technologies

00:24:58.991 --> 00:24:59.861
in the future.

00:25:02.265 --> 00:25:06.960
So the implications continued here
we need to again, appreciate all of

00:25:06.960 --> 00:25:11.813
the requirements and how agencies like
in the UK, the National Institute

00:25:11.813 --> 00:25:15.883
of Clinical Excellence,
how they evaluate technologies and

00:25:15.883 --> 00:25:20.498
we need to collect data in a prospective
way from the very early stage of

00:25:20.498 --> 00:25:25.690
development all the way through clinical
and be aware of those requirements.

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:33.630
Let me briefly go through a case study for
TAVR, transcatheter aortic valve

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:38.000
replacement, remember that we looked at
this technology on the very first slide.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:42.226
And, I'd like to briefly discuss with
you the safety effectiveness and

00:25:42.226 --> 00:25:45.628
value assessment,
based on this real world technology.

00:25:50.236 --> 00:25:55.170
Brief background about the medical
condition aortic valve disease.

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:57.770
The aortic valve is actually
the most important.

00:25:57.770 --> 00:26:01.130
All of the heart valves are important,
but the most important valve or

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:05.460
highest pressure valve, actually between
the left ventricle and the aorta and

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:09.630
the main body artery vasculature.

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:11.560
And in many patients,

00:26:11.560 --> 00:26:15.730
aortic stenosis in older ages leads
to a narrowing of this valve.

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:19.980
In the old days and up to now,

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:24.950
the main treatment approach was open
heart surgery, which is the on top here.

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:30.520
Which requires a very invasive procedure,
open chest, and you can increase the.

00:26:30.520 --> 00:26:34.890
Now what that means in terms of length of
stay, in terms of quality of life impact,

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:36.345
of the mortality impact in fact.

00:26:36.345 --> 00:26:40.450
And transcatheter aortic valve replacement
is a new technology that has been

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:45.000
introduced over the last decade to average

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:49.130
patients that are not candidates for
surgery because they're too sick,

00:26:49.130 --> 00:26:52.140
too ill, and wouldn't survive or
possibly survive surgery.

00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:58.810
And increasingly, this approach is
also used to replace surgeries and

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:01.480
candidates that are at intermediate risk.

00:27:01.480 --> 00:27:06.417
And you can see on this image here on the
right-hand side how the valve is actually

00:27:06.417 --> 00:27:11.004
implanted through the catheter without
opening the chest of the patient.

00:27:14.716 --> 00:27:19.236
So now if we look through
the regulatory or the value lens again,

00:27:19.236 --> 00:27:23.841
it's starting with the question
of general evaluation of TAVR,

00:27:23.841 --> 00:27:29.283
we probably should compare this TAVR
approach against surgical treatment,

00:27:29.283 --> 00:27:33.410
in patients that are candidates for
surgery.

00:27:33.410 --> 00:27:37.220
In patients previously not candidates for
surgery, we need to compare

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:41.950
against the existing paradigm for these
patients, which was medical management.

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:45.940
So again it shows that we
need to carefully present and

00:27:45.940 --> 00:27:49.500
understand what the patient
population that we're looking at when

00:27:49.500 --> 00:27:52.830
evaluating the technology, and also
what the current paradigm of treatment.

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:57.010
Because that drives our incremental
differences in terms of patient outcome,

00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:57.820
and in terms of cost.

00:27:59.050 --> 00:28:02.240
How should we evaluate the effectiveness?

00:28:02.240 --> 00:28:08.460
Well it's the long term survival that
really is the most meaningful factor here.

00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:11.780
So we need to appreciate not
only the procedural success or

00:28:11.780 --> 00:28:16.640
maybe six month follow up outcome but
ideally the remaining lifetime of

00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:21.710
the patient and how survival is
affected by this new technology.

00:28:21.710 --> 00:28:25.640
We also need to think about
events like strokes and

00:28:25.640 --> 00:28:29.120
heart attacks that might be affected
by the use of the technology.

00:28:30.170 --> 00:28:35.110
And to assess those outcomes, ideally,
we would want to see one year or

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:37.899
two year outcomes from a trial
to be able to evaluate that.

00:28:39.850 --> 00:28:43.290
There are also factors like
six minute walk test and

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:47.881
other approaches to evaluate
the functional ability of a patient.

00:28:47.881 --> 00:28:52.650
All of those factors in sum
really drive effectiveness and

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:58.229
do have an impact on the quality of
the That we're measuring and Analysis.

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:04.170
What cost elements do we need to consider
when comparing TAVR and surgical AVR?

00:29:04.170 --> 00:29:07.260
So the main stay treatment,
open chest surgery.

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:11.600
We need to consider, for example,
the number of intensive care days

00:29:11.600 --> 00:29:14.190
that are required after one
versus the other procedure.

00:29:14.190 --> 00:29:16.970
We need to consider length of stay.

00:29:16.970 --> 00:29:21.170
And we need to consider certainly
the cost of the wealth,

00:29:21.170 --> 00:29:26.688
a transcatheter aortic heart valve
actually costs around $30,000

00:29:26.688 --> 00:29:31.770
in the United States versus
maybe 6,000 for a surgical belt.

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:36.060
And you can imagine how that
factors into the cost equation and

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:37.410
the cost difference equation.

00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:41.610
So what clinical data are needed
to support cost calculations?

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:44.388
As I mentioned earlier on
the effectiveness side,

00:29:44.388 --> 00:29:48.459
there are parameters like stroke rates,
and heart attack rates that might

00:29:48.459 --> 00:29:51.860
also be influenced by the use
of this different technology.

00:29:51.860 --> 00:29:57.530
And certainly we want to capture those
also on the cost side as the treatment

00:29:57.530 --> 00:30:01.380
of this drug has immediate cost
implications and long term implications.

00:30:02.580 --> 00:30:06.200
More data sources could be used
to support a cost assessment

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:09.566
where we could do a micro-costing
approach during the trial.

00:30:09.566 --> 00:30:12.950
That there's also extensive literature or

00:30:12.950 --> 00:30:18.010
publication based data available that
discuss the cost of a stroke treatment or

00:30:18.010 --> 00:30:22.120
to discuss the cost of an ICU aid,
intensive care aid.

00:30:22.120 --> 00:30:26.220
But there are a number of sources we
can rely on when performing these

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:27.650
health economic value assessments.

00:30:28.910 --> 00:30:32.900
My therapy difference is between
that groups of patients, absolutely.

00:30:32.900 --> 00:30:37.790
So again it shows that we need
to be aware of the specific

00:30:37.790 --> 00:30:42.370
target population that we're evaluating,
not only from a regulatory perspective but

00:30:42.370 --> 00:30:44.710
also from the health economic perspective.

00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:47.895
And you think of high risk patient or
extreme risk patients for example,

00:30:47.895 --> 00:30:51.860
versus intermediate risk patients,
the safety profile and

00:30:51.860 --> 00:30:55.740
the health economic profile
could look very different.

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:59.830
And you should also appreciate that
when going back to the benefits

00:30:59.830 --> 00:31:01.610
outweighing the risks equation, right.

00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:07.017
Or the scale here that we saw earlier
that certainly the risks in patients that

00:31:07.017 --> 00:31:12.359
are at extreme risk are much different
than patients at intermediate risk.

00:31:12.359 --> 00:31:14.151
Right, so keep that in mind.

00:31:17.543 --> 00:31:21.776
If you're interested in reading more
about the health-economic evaluation of

00:31:21.776 --> 00:31:23.767
a transcatheter aortic heart valve,

00:31:23.767 --> 00:31:26.193
there's an interesting
study by Reynolds and

00:31:26.193 --> 00:31:30.570
colleagues that was published last year
and they give you the reference down here.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:33.550
But it shows what we just looked
at this plane of cost and

00:31:33.550 --> 00:31:37.020
effect, and the likelihood that

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:41.000
the technology actually would be
cost-effective when considering

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:45.430
a threshold of 50 to $150,000 per college
supply period in the United States.

00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:49.990
And the conclusion of that study was even
though technology costs more from a health

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:53.830
care system perspective,
the improved outcomes actually

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:58.560
lead to a very favorable
health-economic profile,

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:03.649
willingness to pay off the society as
stated in the United States between 50 and

00:32:03.649 --> 00:32:08.120
150,000 Means that this should be adopted,
even if it's higher cost.

00:32:11.980 --> 00:32:15.270
There are also a couple of
interesting regulatory documents.

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:19.930
The pre-market approval PMA summary
of the Transcatheter Heart Valve

00:32:19.930 --> 00:32:24.090
device is available under
this link at the agency and

00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:27.340
you find all of the documentation
of the clinical studies and

00:32:27.340 --> 00:32:29.960
pre clinical studies that were performed,
so it's very educational.

00:32:31.180 --> 00:32:34.559
Similarly as I mentioned
the cost effectiveness article.

00:32:37.002 --> 00:32:42.915
So to briefly summarize without regulatory
approval there's no market access The key

00:32:42.915 --> 00:32:48.720
criteria for approval in the United States
are safety and effectiveness.

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:50.680
We need to determine early on,

00:32:50.680 --> 00:32:55.540
how the product will be regulated because
that drives the regulatory requirement.

00:32:55.540 --> 00:33:00.250
We also need to appreciate detailed
requirement with regards to

00:33:00.250 --> 00:33:04.460
clinical testing and so on for
each of the FDA pathways.

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:08.490
And FDA online resources are available
again if you want to study further.

00:33:08.490 --> 00:33:12.510
We can also learn from published
documents of prior approvals and

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:17.240
panel meetings that are published on
the FDA website, a trove of evidence and

00:33:17.240 --> 00:33:20.010
information that really is very helpful.

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:24.342
And as discussed and then a population
appropriate comparative predicate

00:33:24.342 --> 00:33:27.375
endpoints can be gleaned
from those publications.

00:33:29.252 --> 00:33:33.860
QSR, good laboratory practices,
and good manufacturing practices,

00:33:33.860 --> 00:33:38.180
as discussed, are additional
important elements of regulation.

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:45.090
On the health economics side, we have
seen that health economic evaluation

00:33:45.090 --> 00:33:48.780
increasingly is a critical requirement.

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:52.930
Any health care system that wants to make
meaningful decisions about the adoption of

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:57.070
devices and their reimbursement
price needs to evaluate

00:33:57.070 --> 00:34:02.340
the healthcare benefits and the patient
benefits and the cost of the technology.

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:05.360
And that means for us,
from a innovator perspective,

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:06.970
that we need to be aware
of those requirements.

00:34:06.970 --> 00:34:11.800
We need to actually collect information
during the trial and during development,

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:15.770
already collecting evidence that we can
use to demonstrate the value proposition.

00:34:15.770 --> 00:34:20.830
We also need to appreciate what are the
key value drivers of a new therapy,

00:34:20.830 --> 00:34:23.470
very early on in development.

00:34:23.470 --> 00:34:26.880
And we need to appreciate that it's
not only the episode of care or

00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:28.200
episode of implantation, but

00:34:28.200 --> 00:34:32.780
the long-term implications and effects
of the therapy that we need to consider.

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:36.790
Value is defined by incremental changes
from current status quo, so we need to

00:34:36.790 --> 00:34:40.380
appreciate what are the current therapies,
what are the outcomes, and the cost.

00:34:40.380 --> 00:34:43.330
And, again, we have differences
between individual countries.

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:47.330
Useful resources for
health economics are HTAs or

00:34:47.330 --> 00:34:52.010
health technology assessment reports that
are published and publicly available

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:54.730
as well as cost effectiveness
studies in the published literature.

00:34:56.845 --> 00:35:00.425
So, with that, I'd like to briefly
say a few sentences about 256,

00:35:00.425 --> 00:35:06.415
which studying this year actually
has lifted bioengineering course,

00:35:06.415 --> 00:35:10.115
bio e256 tech assessment and
regulation of medical devices.

00:35:10.115 --> 00:35:15.855
It's a course that is given Fridays,
1:30 to 3:20, nine sessions total

00:35:15.855 --> 00:35:21.210
available through SCDB Lectures include
guest speakers from industry and the FDA.

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:25.160
And also, a hands on team based
class project investigating

00:35:25.160 --> 00:35:28.680
a real world medical technology report
from the regulatory perspective but

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:31.850
also on the health-economic
value perspective.

00:35:31.850 --> 00:35:35.277
And you can find details on
the website which is given here.

00:35:37.922 --> 00:35:38.580
Thank you very much.

