he can answer some of the questions
that you've been sending through.

00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:49.450
&gt;&gt; Thank you Marsha.

00:35:49.450 --> 00:35:51.060
Thank you for all of your questions.

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:54.360
We did receive a couple of
them that are listed here.

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:56.310
And let me just go into them step by step.

00:35:57.650 --> 00:36:02.950
One of you asked about the designed
thinking process or concepts and

00:36:02.950 --> 00:36:08.190
how they have been used and
applied in the medical technology field.

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:13.240
And some of you that are not familiar
with designed thinking, it's a process

00:36:13.240 --> 00:36:20.130
that was defined at Stanford and
at IDO Evaluating or using concepts of

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:26.480
use identification, by observation and
those contents have been implemented,

00:36:26.480 --> 00:36:30.531
mainly in the process,
taught at the center,

00:36:30.531 --> 00:36:35.830
that mentioned earlier Her fellowship
is offered that emphasizes

00:36:35.830 --> 00:36:41.130
heavily the early phases of the process
of medical technology innovation,

00:36:41.130 --> 00:36:46.330
where we try to have the fellows spend
time in the actual clinical studying.

00:36:46.330 --> 00:36:52.400
And observe processes, to identify needs,
and then in a very careful manner and

00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:58.360
process narrow the needs down into
the most important needs that then

00:36:58.360 --> 00:37:03.040
lead to the next step which is actually
innovation or inventing technologies and

00:37:03.040 --> 00:37:08.560
then figuring concepts down to one
final product or product idea.

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:11.220
So, the design thinking process is really,

00:37:11.220 --> 00:37:15.680
we think, very aptical with medical
technology and has been used for

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:19.630
50 years in the prior designed program
very efficiently, and effectively.

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:23.170
But, how do you prove, next question,

00:37:23.170 --> 00:37:25.960
how do you prove value on something
like preventative medicine?

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:32.930
A very good question, and indeed there are
important programs that are supported by

00:37:32.930 --> 00:37:38.000
technology also where you try to avoid or
improve the long-term patients outcome for

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:40.480
things, for example,
with weight management and obesity.

00:37:40.480 --> 00:37:43.940
You can think of cardiac risk factors.

00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:47.370
If you manage them effectively, you can
influence the long-term outcome and

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:50.660
the benefit of the technology.

00:37:50.660 --> 00:37:54.880
Are evaluated prospectively and
sometimes in modern day assessment.

00:37:54.880 --> 00:38:01.100
So, you try to appreciate how behavior and
certain parameters

00:38:01.100 --> 00:38:06.419
like blood pressure or weight are affected
with the use of such program.

00:38:06.419 --> 00:38:09.610
And then, you can project
the likely long term impact and

00:38:09.610 --> 00:38:12.860
evaluate whether it would be meaningful,
both in terms of outcome, but

00:38:12.860 --> 00:38:16.280
also in terms of cost, so
a very good question and

00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:19.540
there are many technologies actually
that focus on the parantative field.

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:28.630
There's one question about transcarotid
back replacement here I mentioned

00:38:28.630 --> 00:38:33.660
the roughly $30,000 cost that is published
in publications in the United States.

00:38:34.670 --> 00:38:37.110
And the question is what is the price?

00:38:37.110 --> 00:38:39.820
There are some variations.

00:38:39.820 --> 00:38:47.789
And again, just going back public domain
information in European countries.

00:38:47.789 --> 00:38:52.450
According to publications It's
in the range of 20,000 Euros.

00:38:52.450 --> 00:38:56.800
Again, variation within those countries
but yes, there are global differences.

00:38:56.800 --> 00:39:00.650
But we need to also keep in mind
that healthcare in general and

00:39:00.650 --> 00:39:04.680
healthcare systems costs are very
different in these other markets.

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:06.690
And certainly,

00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:12.840
pricing of technologies impacts the value
proposition of the technology as well.

00:39:12.840 --> 00:39:15.380
These are all very intertwined aspects.

00:39:17.503 --> 00:39:19.977
And we just look at
the next question here,

00:39:19.977 --> 00:39:24.516
how will the changes in development of
medical device regulation in Europe and

00:39:24.516 --> 00:39:28.250
in Asia affect the development
of innovative medical devices?

00:39:28.250 --> 00:39:32.190
For a long time, Europe was seen,

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:36.160
from a regulatory perspective, more
innovation, and that still is the case.

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:41.770
But there are some changes
in regulation on the way,

00:39:41.770 --> 00:39:45.350
currently need to be approved
by the European Parliament,

00:39:45.350 --> 00:39:49.430
to be implemented, but
especially for high risk devices.

00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:54.080
The requirements are now suppose to
be a little bit more stringent and

00:39:54.080 --> 00:39:57.540
also to the PMA evaluation
in the United States.

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:03.330
In Asia there are changes,
in Japan it was significant.

00:40:04.650 --> 00:40:07.370
There were significant issues
with a review process in that it

00:40:07.370 --> 00:40:08.450
took a very long time.

00:40:08.450 --> 00:40:12.700
And you can imagine that very long review
time means also delayed patient exits.

00:40:12.700 --> 00:40:17.790
And the Japanese had their authorities
that worked hard in improving

00:40:17.790 --> 00:40:22.140
a more rapid review while maintaining
high levels of safety and effectiveness.

00:40:22.140 --> 00:40:27.802
But certainly those factors of global
regulatory systems also influence

00:40:27.802 --> 00:40:33.829
companies' strategic decision-making
about initial markets to access and

00:40:33.829 --> 00:40:38.139
gain clinical experiences with,
and commercialize.

00:40:38.139 --> 00:40:41.996
And I think there's one more question
here about doctor regulations can be,

00:40:41.996 --> 00:40:44.860
would I talk a little bit more
about doctor regulations.

00:40:44.860 --> 00:40:49.391
Certainly, when you look at
the technologies we've evaluated, or

00:40:49.391 --> 00:40:52.594
looked at, early on,
deep brain stimulation,

00:40:52.594 --> 00:40:56.968
heavily driven by doctor right when
you think of an ECG machine, or

00:40:56.968 --> 00:41:01.993
even this mammography device that we
looked at, software plays a big role.

00:41:01.993 --> 00:41:06.572
And FDA has guidelines for software
regulation, and for software testing, and

00:41:06.572 --> 00:41:09.450
for failure modes and effect analysis.

00:41:09.450 --> 00:41:16.240
So they are a key cornerstone of the
review process, certainly not an easy one.

00:41:16.240 --> 00:41:21.030
And I think their continued
efforts to study better or develop

00:41:21.030 --> 00:41:25.530
better approaches to evaluate software and
determine how much radiation and

00:41:25.530 --> 00:41:31.200
testing do you need to do of doctor to
make sure that it's reliable enough.

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:33.940
Something that is not only limited
to the health care industry,

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:37.040
but also to other industries
like automotive and

00:41:37.040 --> 00:41:40.560
aerospace where you face the same
issues of software reliability.

00:41:42.636 --> 00:41:47.090
&gt;&gt; I'll jump in with one that came in a
